[{:seon.task/name "a-repeated-defect-writes-nothing"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists (merged): a signature already written to main is not written again for one more occurrence. `seon.error/fold` keeps the repeat count in the observer's fold: an entry carries :base once written, a repeat sets :pending? (not :dirty?); `seon.error/flush-tx` writes :dirty? entries and, when it writes any, the pending counts with them; `seon.root/flush-problems` sends nothing for an empty tx; `seon.problems/report` takes the fold (`seon.root/errors`) for true counts. Reproduction it fixed (a clone of the standing store, LMDB map full at 1 GiB): one recorded error had `:seon.error/count` 41,709 in 70.5 s, 592 commits/s, 15 MB/s, about 6 nodes / 13-28 KB of file per commit, 99.99% of the file unreachable from any head. The cause was a feedback loop closed by the error writer: a main observer (`seon.reconcile/reconciler-proc`'s :main step) threw on every commit (a Flow step that throws keeps its state, so its :t stayed behind, docs/flow.md), root folded and flushed the error as a commit, and that commit was the observer's next input. Rejected: advancing the reconciler's :t on a failed step (hides the loop in one proc) and throttling the writer (a clock decides). v1 recorded Seon's own errors once per signature at main, with a count; repeats only count (~/src/seon CLAUDE.md). Not done: the pending counts are not flushed at root's stop (the writer's port closes in the step that would write them). Separate, not fixed here: `seon.error/flush-tx` stores the first example as an unbounded `pr-str` (4.5 KB, 2 of the 6 nodes rewritten per commit); stored text should be the one printer's value within its budget. Estimate: an idle system writes 0 commits per repeat; the first record is one commit of about 6 nodes."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.problems-test/a-written-signature-is-not-written-again
                      seon.problems-test/a-defect-in-the-reconciler-does-not-feed-itself}}]
